<?xml version="1.0"?>
<section xml:id="basic-ds_the-unordered-list-abstract-data-type" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>The Unordered List Abstract Data Type</title>
  <p>The structure of an unordered list, as described above, is a collection
    of items where each item holds a relative position with respect to the
    others. The default list in Kotlin is an unordered list. It has a very large collection of member functions, but we'll focus on a small number here as an example.</p>
  <p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p><c>add(item)</c> adds a new item to the end of the list making it the last item in the collection. It needs the item and returns nothing.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p><c>add(index, item)</c> adds a new item to the list at the provided index, inserting it before the remaining items in the list. <c>index</c> may not be bigger than the length of the list (in order to prevent empty entries in the list). It needs the item and returns nothing.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p><c>get(index)</c> returns the item in the list at position <c>index</c>. The index must be at least 0 and no more than one less than the length of the list; otherwise, an exception is thrown.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p><c>indexOf(item)</c> searches for the item in the list and returns the first index where it is found. It needs the item and returns the index of the item, or a -1 if not found.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p><c>isEmpty()</c> tests to see whether the list is empty. It needs no parameters and returns a boolean value.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p><c>removeAt(index, item)</c> removes the item from the list at position `index`. It needs the index and modifies the list. The index must be at least 0 and no more than one less than the length of the list; otherwise, an exception is thrown. Items at higher index values are shifted down to fill the gap. It returns nothing.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p><c>size()</c> returns the number of items in the list. It needs no parameters and returns an integer.</p>
      </li>

    </ul>
  </p>

  <aside>
    <p>Note that we have two different functions `add`, each of which takes a different set of parameters. This is called <em>overloading</em>. When one of these functions is called, Kotlin can figure out which one is intended based on the number and types of the parameters used when calling the function. </p>
  </aside>

  <p>Here is an interface representing an unordered list:</p>

